    "The label provides percentages from a U.S. clinical trial of 141 patients receiving metformin (up to 2550 mg/day) and 145 receiving placebo. Adverse reactions occurring in >5% of metformin‑treated patients and more often than placebo were: diarrhea (53% vs 12% placebo), nausea/vomiting (26% vs 8%), flatulence (12% vs 6%), asthenia (9% vs 6%), indigestion (7% vs 4%), abdominal discomfort (6% vs 5%) and headache (6% vs 5%). Diarrhea led to discontinuation in 6% of metformin patients. In a 29‑week trial, about 7% of patients experienced a decrease of previously normal serum vitamin B12 to subnormal levels. The label also notes other reactions reported in ≤1% to ≤45% of patients (e.g., hypoglycemia, rash) without giving exact percentages. [openFDA label:FDA-LABEL-metformin-2]",
    "The post‑marketing section lists cholestatic, hepatocellular, and mixed hepatocellular liver injury as adverse reactions that have been reported, but it explicitly states that these reports are voluntary and come from a population of uncertain size, so a reliable incidence or frequency cannot be derived from the label. Therefore, the label cannot tell us how often liver injury occurs in real‑world use. [openFDA label:FDA-LABEL-metformin-2]"
  ],
